1
00:00:00,777 --> 00:00:11,301
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought we could continue with the Majjhima Nikaya series, but skipping ahead quite a bit to discourse number 131.

2
00:00:11,321 --> 00:00:17,283
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's actually... there's four discourses that are the same.

3
00:00:17,303 --> 00:00:18,584
[SPEAKER_00]: 132, 133, 134.

4
00:00:20,384 --> 00:00:21,825
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's called One Good Night.

5
00:00:25,306 --> 00:00:29,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll read the verse that is found in each of those suttas.

6
00:00:33,327 --> 00:00:37,688
[SPEAKER_00]: One should not dwell on the past nor hope for the future.

7
00:00:38,969 --> 00:00:42,030
[SPEAKER_00]: The past is over and the future is yet to arrive.

8
00:00:43,470 --> 00:00:51,313
[SPEAKER_00]: One who sees clearly presently arisen things should train their mind to not be caught or moved there.

9
00:00:53,033 --> 00:00:57,174
[SPEAKER_00]: That task should be done today, for tomorrow death may come.

10
00:00:58,315 --> 00:01:02,216
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no negotiating with death's mighty force.

11
00:01:04,213 --> 00:01:12,997
[SPEAKER_00]: By living day and night with such uncompromising effort, the peaceful sage has declared one to have a good night.

12
00:01:15,938 --> 00:01:21,881
[SPEAKER_00]: And that verse is found in each of those suttas, repeated in just... in different contexts.

13
00:01:25,502 --> 00:01:29,744
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the questions that follow is, how does one

14
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:34,541
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, chase after the past.

15
00:01:37,741 --> 00:01:43,723
[SPEAKER_00]: And it says, one delights in thinking, I had such a form, I had such a feeling, I had such...

16
00:01:44,303 --> 00:01:46,323
[SPEAKER_00]: I had such aggregates, I had such experiences.

17
00:01:46,623 --> 00:01:47,683
[SPEAKER_03]: I had such experiences.

18
00:01:47,783 --> 00:01:49,864
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, it was like this back in the day, and...

19
00:01:50,844 --> 00:01:55,605
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... delighting in those... that's how you... Yeah.

20
00:01:56,765 --> 00:01:59,146
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as is often heard,

21
00:02:00,669 --> 00:02:04,572
[SPEAKER_00]: In spiritual circles, it's like, don't think about the past.

22
00:02:05,313 --> 00:02:11,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And it comes kind of... this kind of verse can make it sound like, don't... sure, forget about the past.

23
00:02:12,619 --> 00:02:13,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Forget about it.

24
00:02:15,562 --> 00:02:20,246
[SPEAKER_00]: But this is thinking, you know, does say thinking, delighting.

25
00:02:21,487 --> 00:02:24,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Delighting in those past things.

26
00:02:26,231 --> 00:02:27,572
[SPEAKER_02]: Almost nostalgic about

27
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:30,740
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a bit subtler than that.

28
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:35,381
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just being nostalgic.

29
00:02:39,762 --> 00:02:41,183
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you stop thinking about the past?

30
00:02:42,243 --> 00:02:43,924
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you do something?

31
00:02:43,964 --> 00:02:47,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Memories are part of you.

32
00:02:47,744 --> 00:02:49,985
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

33
00:02:50,945 --> 00:02:57,847
[SPEAKER_03]: So, see, when people try to... People should stop dwelling through delight on the past.

34
00:02:59,432 --> 00:03:01,093
[SPEAKER_03]: People should stop taking past for granted.

35
00:03:04,136 --> 00:03:10,942
[SPEAKER_03]: But it doesn't mean you should stop thinking about it, as if that's an option, that you could just stop thinking about it and not think about the past.

36
00:03:11,743 --> 00:03:13,084
[SPEAKER_03]: You could choose not to dwell on it.

37
00:03:13,204 --> 00:03:14,565
[SPEAKER_03]: You could choose not to delight in it.

38
00:03:14,605 --> 00:03:15,766
[SPEAKER_03]: You could choose not to welcome it.

39
00:03:17,068 --> 00:03:19,490
[SPEAKER_03]: But you can't choose not to think it, because you are it.

40
00:03:22,392 --> 00:03:26,276
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when people say stop thinking about the past, they overshoot the mark.

41
00:03:27,290 --> 00:03:36,256
[SPEAKER_03]: Because they want to stop dwelling on it, but they can't see the middle ground where dwelling stops without trying to get rid of it, and then they just go too far and try to get rid of it.

42
00:03:39,198 --> 00:03:43,541
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you find the middle ground where the past is?

43
00:03:43,561 --> 00:03:45,022
[SPEAKER_03]: Do you need to go back into the past?

44
00:03:47,663 --> 00:03:48,444
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because that's...

45
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:50,961
[SPEAKER_03]: So, say you're thinking about the past.

46
00:03:51,181 --> 00:03:56,083
[SPEAKER_03]: You're thinking about a past event that your mind wants to delight in.

47
00:03:56,224 --> 00:03:58,985
[SPEAKER_03]: It was so great, and you want to relive it again.

48
00:04:00,706 --> 00:04:01,867
[SPEAKER_03]: Where is that experience?

49
00:04:01,967 --> 00:04:03,127
[SPEAKER_00]: It's presently arisen.

50
00:04:03,247 --> 00:04:03,908
[SPEAKER_03]: It's right now.

51
00:04:04,168 --> 00:04:04,808
[SPEAKER_03]: It's right here.

52
00:04:05,188 --> 00:04:06,749
[SPEAKER_03]: That is its framework.

53
00:04:06,889 --> 00:04:11,612
[SPEAKER_03]: Doesn't matter how far in the past you go, it is presently... like you're presently thinking it.

54
00:04:12,867 --> 00:04:13,948
[SPEAKER_03]: It can't be any other way.

55
00:04:14,528 --> 00:04:23,493
[SPEAKER_03]: But see, when you overlook the fact that you're presently thinking any past possible, when you overlook that fact, you are in the past.

56
00:04:25,514 --> 00:04:32,858
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, your thoughts imply this was back then, in that time, in that place, not here anymore.

57
00:04:34,278 --> 00:04:37,940
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't see the here-ness, so to speak, of it, still here.

58
00:04:38,641 --> 00:04:40,381
[SPEAKER_03]: Because here you're thinking it.

59
00:04:40,502 --> 00:04:41,322
[SPEAKER_03]: Now you're thinking it.

60
00:04:42,730 --> 00:04:43,971
[SPEAKER_03]: And it always has been that way.

61
00:04:45,192 --> 00:04:56,338
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you begin by not losing that perspective... From that presently... That whatever past you might be thinking, it can only be thought presently.

62
00:04:56,498 --> 00:04:57,659
[SPEAKER_03]: There is no past thinking.

63
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:00,761
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, apply the same in the future.

64
00:05:01,501 --> 00:05:07,625
[SPEAKER_03]: Any future thought that you're planning for tomorrow, see, if you overlook the fact that that is present here now, while you're thinking it,

65
00:05:08,954 --> 00:05:15,476
[SPEAKER_03]: You will imply that those thoughts are the thoughts of tomorrow and for tomorrow, and they're over there, and they're more in the future than they're here.

66
00:05:16,156 --> 00:05:17,997
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why you start blaming the thoughts.

67
00:05:18,037 --> 00:05:19,457
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, stop thinking about the future.

68
00:05:19,597 --> 00:05:21,518
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, don't think about the past.

69
00:05:22,158 --> 00:05:23,758
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, those are just thoughts in the present.

70
00:05:24,298 --> 00:05:25,219
[SPEAKER_03]: Why would it be a problem?

71
00:05:27,779 --> 00:05:33,961
[SPEAKER_03]: So, once you see that whether it's the past, whether it's the future, whether it's the present, it can only be thought presently.

72
00:05:35,402 --> 00:05:36,682
[SPEAKER_03]: You are, then,

73
00:05:37,588 --> 00:05:41,669
[SPEAKER_03]: Completely aligned with what verses say you should do.

74
00:05:43,669 --> 00:05:48,050
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's not like, oh, I shall do these things presently.

75
00:05:48,070 --> 00:05:50,750
[SPEAKER_03]: No, you just need to stop overlooking that they're already present.

76
00:05:51,591 --> 00:05:52,551
[SPEAKER_03]: It can't be any other way.

77
00:05:54,331 --> 00:06:03,453
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's another thing that people, they would be blaming the thoughts, blaming the past, blaming the future, stop thinking about it, don't dwell on it, and so on, just just just be in the present moment.

78
00:06:04,273 --> 00:06:05,293
[SPEAKER_03]: That's another thing they miss.

79
00:06:06,508 --> 00:06:09,849
[SPEAKER_03]: The fact that you can't be anywhere else except in the present moment.

80
00:06:09,889 --> 00:06:14,550
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it does not need to come from your volitional effort of being present.

81
00:06:15,990 --> 00:06:18,291
[SPEAKER_03]: If it actually comes from that effort, you are not present.

82
00:06:18,591 --> 00:06:21,412
[SPEAKER_03]: You are not aware of that effort being the present moment.

83
00:06:22,792 --> 00:06:27,413
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're doing the present moment on your own terms, through your own craving, through your own notions.

84
00:06:28,374 --> 00:06:28,514
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

85
00:06:28,634 --> 00:06:32,115
[SPEAKER_03]: You find the framework of the present moment, which is already there.

86
00:06:33,015 --> 00:06:33,955
[SPEAKER_03]: And you are put second.

87
00:06:36,997 --> 00:06:40,939
[SPEAKER_03]: Any notion of me, I was, I will be, it's second to it.

88
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:53,544
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why the Buddha would always convey that by saying, oh, he does not dwell and think like, oh, I was in the past, I was such in the past, I had such form, I, I, me, me, me.

89
00:06:54,765 --> 00:07:02,788
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you still think like that, even if you think the present moment, you're thinking it through me, me, me, me, me, which is not really the framework of the present moment.

90
00:07:09,083 --> 00:07:12,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how does one anticipate the future?

91
00:07:12,767 --> 00:07:18,012
[SPEAKER_00]: One delights in thinking, may I have such an experience, such a form, such a feeling, such a perception, and so on.

92
00:07:19,393 --> 00:07:24,218
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, how is one caught by presently arisen states?

93
00:07:24,518 --> 00:07:25,979
[SPEAKER_00]: How is one caught and moved?

94
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:31,905
[SPEAKER_00]: It says here, an ordinary person, who is unskilled in the noble dhamma and discipline,

95
00:07:32,715 --> 00:07:42,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, his sense of self, his I am, is the center point, is the reference for any permutation.

96
00:07:42,241 --> 00:07:44,603
[SPEAKER_00]: The self has to be there, one way or the other.

97
00:07:44,623 --> 00:07:45,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

98
00:07:58,713 --> 00:08:01,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even... so, whatever you are experiencing, you're caught there.

99
00:08:02,214 --> 00:08:03,615
[SPEAKER_03]: But you could... you could ask the same.

100
00:08:03,655 --> 00:08:11,357
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, when you think about yourself, whatever ambiguous thoughts you might have, or images or notions, there it is.

101
00:08:11,377 --> 00:08:13,038
[SPEAKER_03]: What are those thoughts, other than thoughts?

102
00:08:13,198 --> 00:08:13,398
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

103
00:08:14,719 --> 00:08:16,259
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like, oh, so the self is not real.

104
00:08:16,299 --> 00:08:16,459
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

105
00:08:16,479 --> 00:08:24,002
[SPEAKER_03]: The self is real, as a thought, as an image, that you've been misconceiving and attributing far more than it actually is.

106
00:08:25,795 --> 00:08:28,996
[SPEAKER_03]: But it... yeah, you have the genuine notion of I am.

107
00:08:30,156 --> 00:08:30,916
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a real thing.

108
00:08:31,837 --> 00:08:32,957
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's an image as such.

109
00:08:33,237 --> 00:08:33,717
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a thought.

110
00:08:34,017 --> 00:08:38,619
[SPEAKER_03]: It's presently being enduring there and you're thinking it, you're perceiving it as an image.

111
00:08:39,799 --> 00:08:40,919
[SPEAKER_03]: That is its existence.

112
00:08:41,679 --> 00:08:53,783
[SPEAKER_03]: Any other implication that that image might have of me, importance, my own, I am, all of that is now second to the mere image of it.

113
00:08:56,510 --> 00:08:58,230
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's how you start correcting that order.

114
00:08:59,091 --> 00:09:03,152
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you... and you won't be getting caught in thoughts of the past.

115
00:09:03,412 --> 00:09:03,672
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

116
00:09:03,932 --> 00:09:05,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Or thinking about... Less and less.

117
00:09:05,973 --> 00:09:06,993
[SPEAKER_03]: You'll be caught less and less.

118
00:09:07,693 --> 00:09:10,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not caught here and now.

119
00:09:11,534 --> 00:09:11,874
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

120
00:09:11,954 --> 00:09:14,935
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have that view that yourself is beyond or...

121
00:09:15,275 --> 00:09:21,397
[SPEAKER_03]: If you're here, not seeing yourself as an arisen thing that endures on its own, as an image, as a thought,

122
00:09:23,263 --> 00:09:33,272
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you will be implying yourself in the past, and you will be anticipating yourself in the future, because that is its gateway, that is where it comes through.

123
00:09:34,773 --> 00:09:38,376
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, all right, and as it's...

124
00:09:38,436 --> 00:09:40,958
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the... that task should be done today?

125
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:44,641
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, because when else can you do it?

126
00:09:44,701 --> 00:09:44,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

127
00:09:47,363 --> 00:09:49,005
[SPEAKER_00]: For tomorrow death may come, as already

128
00:09:51,317 --> 00:09:53,018
[SPEAKER_00]: There is still a counter, you know.

129
00:09:53,298 --> 00:09:55,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Death is going to happen.

130
00:09:57,519 --> 00:10:06,382
[SPEAKER_00]: And the task... One might think, well, what is the... what is the task there?

131
00:10:06,402 --> 00:10:07,483
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no instruction here.

132
00:10:07,763 --> 00:10:08,143
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no...

133
00:10:08,843 --> 00:10:13,985
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's just, one who sees clearly presently arisen things should train the mind to not be caught.

134
00:10:14,826 --> 00:10:15,066
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

135
00:10:15,086 --> 00:10:18,487
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically, the sutta is instructed for those who have the right view.

136
00:10:19,215 --> 00:10:22,177
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, who sees... Who sees correctly present there isn't things.

137
00:10:22,237 --> 00:10:23,798
[SPEAKER_03]: Not somebody who's still stabbing in the dark.

138
00:10:25,339 --> 00:10:25,940
[SPEAKER_03]: And they...

139
00:10:26,260 --> 00:10:32,384
[SPEAKER_03]: They who see dhammas as dhammas, who see things correctly, who have the right view, should then make the effort to not be caught.

140
00:10:33,785 --> 00:10:43,372
[SPEAKER_03]: To not slip up, to not lose the context, and start carelessly thinking about their form in the past, anticipating in the future, dwelling on it in the present.

141
00:10:46,514 --> 00:10:47,375
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, what is...

142
00:10:51,006 --> 00:10:53,327
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing an image.

143
00:10:53,347 --> 00:10:56,107
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you know you're seeing an image, seeing dhammas as dhammas?

144
00:10:56,768 --> 00:11:01,789
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you know you're seeing things as they are, or presently a risen thing?

145
00:11:02,229 --> 00:11:03,289
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, are you free from suffering?

146
00:11:10,571 --> 00:11:16,993
[SPEAKER_03]: Is there no... that sharp tip of pain that can get you, regardless of the pain, regardless of the circumstances?

147
00:11:17,433 --> 00:11:18,654
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of the thing that's...

148
00:11:18,734 --> 00:11:20,354
[SPEAKER_03]: Regardless of the thing that's risen, yeah.

149
00:11:22,600 --> 00:11:22,780
Yeah.

150
00:11:23,721 --> 00:11:30,384
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's not a metaphysical or a theoretical or abstract notion.

151
00:11:30,484 --> 00:11:38,468
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, obviously, it is for people, but, you know, seeing dhammas as dhammas means basically being able to see your mind as mind.

152
00:11:41,890 --> 00:11:44,031
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's already a prerequisite for satipaṭṭhāna.

153
00:11:44,051 --> 00:11:45,351
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you see your mind as mind?

154
00:11:45,391 --> 00:11:47,752
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, we covered that in many other suttas.

155
00:11:47,993 --> 00:11:48,713
[SPEAKER_03]: You gotta first

156
00:11:50,694 --> 00:11:51,295
[SPEAKER_03]: Round it up.

157
00:11:51,935 --> 00:11:52,536
[SPEAKER_03]: Fence it off.

158
00:11:53,096 --> 00:11:53,757
[SPEAKER_03]: Tie it down.

159
00:11:55,058 --> 00:11:55,999
[SPEAKER_03]: Let it kick and scream.

160
00:11:56,359 --> 00:12:00,563
[SPEAKER_03]: Urinate and defecate in anger and threats, because you're restraining it.

161
00:12:01,444 --> 00:12:10,032
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, when it calms down, then, basically, you present it with things that arise, and start training it to get used to it.

162
00:12:11,513 --> 00:12:13,535
[SPEAKER_03]: Get used to the context that you don't want to think about.

163
00:12:14,195 --> 00:12:17,058
[SPEAKER_03]: Get used to the inevitabilities that you distract yourself from.

164
00:12:18,315 --> 00:12:21,677
[SPEAKER_03]: But first, you need to have tamed it down through virtue and sense restraint.

165
00:12:22,397 --> 00:12:29,221
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to be able to simply put up with saying no to the desires, no to ill will, no to distraction.

166
00:12:30,902 --> 00:12:38,166
[SPEAKER_03]: And once it's able to do that, you bring up the context of sickness, death, various contemplations in the suttas.

167
00:12:39,066 --> 00:12:43,549
[SPEAKER_03]: Not for some great resolution expectation, as I said in the previous talk,

168
00:12:44,488 --> 00:12:48,911
[SPEAKER_03]: But simply bring it up, so the mind can look at it, and then see what the mind does.

169
00:12:49,312 --> 00:12:49,532
[SPEAKER_03]: Same.

170
00:12:49,932 --> 00:12:53,254
[SPEAKER_03]: You want to train a dog, or, like the suttas say, you train a battle elephant.

171
00:12:54,135 --> 00:12:58,918
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't immediately throw it into the battle, but you don't, like, keep it sheltered from the spears either.

172
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:09,846
[SPEAKER_03]: So, once it's there, obeying you to an extent, then, yes, you put some weight on it, see what it does, see whether then it wants to run back to the wilderness.

173
00:13:10,727 --> 00:13:11,568
[SPEAKER_03]: Then correct it.

174
00:13:12,217 --> 00:13:22,802
[SPEAKER_03]: When he gets used to it, then they start prodding it with sticks, then with spears, then with all these little things to train it to simply remain there composed and unperturbed.

175
00:13:23,003 --> 00:13:27,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically thoughts of deaths, thoughts of the inevitability.

176
00:13:27,985 --> 00:13:31,087
[SPEAKER_03]: Thoughts of inevitability, thoughts of things you don't want to think about, basically.

177
00:13:31,227 --> 00:13:37,510
[SPEAKER_03]: Losing your loved ones, losing your wealth, losing your health, losing your life.

178
00:13:37,650 --> 00:13:40,912
[SPEAKER_03]: Just things you're generally... things people are generally intoxicated with.

179
00:13:41,832 --> 00:13:45,354
[SPEAKER_03]: You could just start thinking about it, and probably would have some effect.

180
00:13:46,115 --> 00:13:57,561
[SPEAKER_03]: But generally, if your behavior is still contradicting these very notions of... of kind of dispassion and impermanence, you won't get very far.

181
00:13:57,781 --> 00:14:00,463
[SPEAKER_03]: You might just wind yourself up and become neurotic, basically.

182
00:14:01,243 --> 00:14:03,645
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you do start saying no to your desires,

183
00:14:05,663 --> 00:14:09,024
[SPEAKER_03]: Acting out of ill will, keeping the precepts, restraining yourself.

184
00:14:09,544 --> 00:14:11,144
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, as I said, the mind is contained.

185
00:14:11,844 --> 00:14:13,785
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if it doesn't feel pleasant, it's contained.

186
00:14:14,885 --> 00:14:20,666
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, when these things come up, these thoughts, these contexts, you don't try to get rid of them.

187
00:14:21,026 --> 00:14:22,106
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't distract yourself.

188
00:14:22,806 --> 00:14:28,327
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't fall into views of psychologizing them and explaining the reasons why these thoughts came up.

189
00:14:29,047 --> 00:14:31,988
[SPEAKER_03]: You just leave them there as a cloud hanging over a village.

190
00:14:32,866 --> 00:14:35,287
[SPEAKER_03]: While everybody in the village continues about the day.

191
00:14:36,768 --> 00:14:41,251
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you leave this context in front of the animal, and let it continue, you know.

192
00:14:41,551 --> 00:14:44,733
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, obviously, it's a bit freer, because it's sort of semi-tamed.

193
00:14:45,593 --> 00:14:51,037
[SPEAKER_03]: You let it move, and you watch its reactions, until it gets used to it, until it doesn't care whether the cloud is there or not.

194
00:14:51,997 --> 00:14:53,078
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you bring a bigger cloud.

195
00:14:54,579 --> 00:14:55,719
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you amplify the cloud.

196
00:14:57,340 --> 00:14:59,101
[SPEAKER_03]: And the point will come when nothing will move it.

197
00:15:01,348 --> 00:15:01,989
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the training.

198
00:15:02,089 --> 00:15:04,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Training the mind not to be moved.

199
00:15:05,492 --> 00:15:16,724
[SPEAKER_03]: And the more immovable the mind is, the clearer the images are, the clearer the thoughts are, the clearer the nature of the past is, the clearer the nature of anticipation is.

200
00:15:16,824 --> 00:15:18,506
[SPEAKER_03]: All of that just starts falling into place.

201
00:15:20,025 --> 00:15:26,107
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not really like a complex logical secret that you need to decipher, you know, to understand it.

202
00:15:26,127 --> 00:15:31,809
[SPEAKER_03]: The reason it does not land where it should land is because the mind is nowhere near being tamed.

203
00:15:33,349 --> 00:15:39,711
[SPEAKER_03]: So it can't bear those images, let alone see through them and their implications and their nature.

204
00:15:41,192 --> 00:15:41,852
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not used to.

205
00:15:44,210 --> 00:15:46,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Just bearing with little discomfort.

206
00:15:47,412 --> 00:15:47,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

207
00:15:48,253 --> 00:15:51,015
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not used to bearing just even a little bit.

208
00:15:51,035 --> 00:15:51,555
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, no.

209
00:15:51,655 --> 00:15:54,778
[SPEAKER_03]: It might be used to bearing a lot of discomfort on the physical level.

210
00:15:55,478 --> 00:15:59,021
[SPEAKER_03]: But bearing discomfort is not the same as enduring on the right level, when I say...

211
00:15:59,221 --> 00:16:03,164
[SPEAKER_03]: I keep saying that because people keep confusing it.

212
00:16:03,384 --> 00:16:12,711
[SPEAKER_03]: The point is, enduring on the right level means enduring that certain mental ambiguity slash anxiety slash discomfort only on that certain mental level.

213
00:16:13,587 --> 00:16:18,752
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why the Buddha himself said, no, sometimes I eat two bowls of food and sleep in king's palaces.

214
00:16:19,753 --> 00:16:21,334
[SPEAKER_03]: So he endures no discomfort.

215
00:16:21,975 --> 00:16:28,821
[SPEAKER_03]: And he has disciples that would be eating just like a grain of rice and sleep under a tree and wear raggloves.

216
00:16:28,881 --> 00:16:30,122
[SPEAKER_03]: Yet, they still respect him.

217
00:16:31,143 --> 00:16:32,484
[SPEAKER_03]: They still value his teachings.

218
00:16:32,624 --> 00:16:35,327
[SPEAKER_03]: It's obviously not because of bearing the elements on its own.

219
00:16:36,128 --> 00:16:38,490
[SPEAKER_03]: It's because they understood what the right level is.

220
00:16:40,174 --> 00:16:44,116
[SPEAKER_03]: According to his teaching, and that is what's freeing them, and that's why they respect him.

221
00:16:45,037 --> 00:16:47,198
[SPEAKER_03]: And that right level is that mental level.

222
00:16:48,499 --> 00:17:03,788
[SPEAKER_03]: And as I said many times, like, people would rather be putting up with far worse, factually worse, physical pains, torment, enduring that on that physical level, than to have just like a nagging thought of doubt.

223
00:17:04,608 --> 00:17:06,229
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, what do they think of me?

224
00:17:06,589 --> 00:17:07,550
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, for example, or

225
00:17:08,717 --> 00:17:11,439
[SPEAKER_03]: Was that what, you know, what did he mean when he said that?

226
00:17:11,779 --> 00:17:16,603
[SPEAKER_03]: These little unresolved things, these little mental pressures, that's more unbearable.

227
00:17:16,743 --> 00:17:18,084
[SPEAKER_03]: That will make people go crazy.

228
00:17:18,865 --> 00:17:20,846
[SPEAKER_03]: That usually those things come up in solitude.

229
00:17:21,667 --> 00:17:22,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, it does.

230
00:17:22,888 --> 00:17:34,496
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I would rather just mindlessly you just beat me and give me some some physical bearing task, sit immovable for 10 hours and cry through pain, but mentally I can switch off.

231
00:17:35,137 --> 00:17:36,358
[SPEAKER_03]: Mentally I will switch off.

232
00:17:37,185 --> 00:17:46,169
[SPEAKER_03]: But having a doubt or a worry or an anxiety waking me up at night, 24-7, oh, that's far worse.

233
00:17:46,710 --> 00:17:47,830
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's the right level.

234
00:17:48,791 --> 00:18:01,517
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you're practicing on that level, building it up through right taming, as I said, precepts, sense restraint, watching your speech, avoiding entertainment, moderation in eating, increasing solitude,

235
00:18:02,555 --> 00:18:04,438
[SPEAKER_03]: You wouldn't need to do any of the other stuff.

236
00:18:04,478 --> 00:18:08,924
[SPEAKER_03]: You wouldn't even... You probably occasionally might to bear some elements just to see if anything will come up.

237
00:18:09,405 --> 00:18:11,608
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's going to be insignificant and miniscule.

